  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040771/assets/0775eb4f214e6d4c0388817e59f775d306e647779fe7fb98c856a5a1ab79cb09.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=-2{x}^{2}-9$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}-9)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-9{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-9\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0)-9(2-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}-18 \\\\  & =-\\frac{70}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{70}{3}.$",
